### Library API - Test Requests
### Branch: 1-myfirstlibrary (SENZA Repository Pattern)

### Variables
@baseUrl = http://localhost:5000

### 1. Get all books
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/books
Accept: application/json

### 2. Get book by ID
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/books/1
Accept: application/json

### 3. Get book by ID (not found)
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/books/999
Accept: application/json

### 4. Search books by title
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/books/search?title=gatsby
Accept: application/json

### 5. Search books by author
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/books/search?author=orwell
Accept: application/json

### 6. Search books by title and author
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/books/search?title=code&author=martin
Accept: application/json

### 7. Create new book
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/books
Content-Type: application/json

{
    "title": "Domain-Driven Design",
    "author": "Eric Evans",
    "isbn": "978-0-321-12521-7",
    "publishedDate": "2003-08-20T00:00:00",
    "genre": "Technology",
    "availableCopies": 3,
    "price": 59.99
}

### 8. Create book with duplicate ISBN (should fail)
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/books
Content-Type: application/json

{
    "title": "Duplicate ISBN Test",
    "author": "Test Author",
    "isbn": "978-0-7432-7356-5",
    "publishedDate": "2024-01-01T00:00:00",
    "genre": "Test",
    "availableCopies": 1,
    "price": 9.99
}

### 9. Create book with invalid data (should fail)
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/books
Content-Type: application/json

{
    "title": "",
    "author": "Test Author",
    "isbn": "978-1-111-11111-1",
    "publishedDate": "2024-01-01T00:00:00",
    "genre": "Test",
    "availableCopies": -1,
    "price": 9.99
}

### 10. Update existing book
PUT {{baseUrl}}/api/books/1
Content-Type: application/json

{
    "id": 1,
    "title": "The Great Gatsby - Updated Edition",
    "author": "F. Scott Fitzgerald",
    "isbn": "978-0-7432-7356-5",
    "publishedDate": "1925-04-10T00:00:00",
    "genre": "Classic Fiction",
    "availableCopies": 8,
    "price": 15.99
}

### 11. Update non-existing book (should fail)
PUT {{baseUrl}}/api/books/999
Content-Type: application/json

{
    "id": 999,
    "title": "Non Existing Book",
    "author": "Unknown Author",
    "isbn": "978-9-999-99999-9",
    "publishedDate": "2024-01-01T00:00:00",
    "genre": "Test",
    "availableCopies": 1,
    "price": 9.99
}

### 12. Delete book (first check which books have no active loans)
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/api/books/8

### 13. Try to delete book with active loans (should fail)
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/api/books/2

### 14. Delete non-existing book (should fail)
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/api/books/999

###
### Nota per gli studenti:
### 
### Osservate come tutte queste operazioni sono gestite direttamente nel controller.
### Nel prossimo branch (2-mysecondlibrary) vedremo come il Repository Pattern
### può migliorare l'architettura separando le responsabilità.
###
### Problemi evidenti:
### 1. Logica di business nel controller (validazioni, controlli duplicati)
### 2. Query SQL embedded nel controller
### 3. Gestione errori database nel controller
### 4. Impossibilità di testare senza database reale
### 5. Accoppiamento forte con Dapper e database specifici
###